and the second is active retrieval.

870af4ba-51bb-4229-8d02-12d91c82ddbc-0
00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:31.999
So it turns out that forgetting something
is actually a necessary part of forming

870af4ba-51bb-4229-8d02-12d91c82ddbc-1
00:10:31.999 --> 00:10:37.204
memories that can be reliably accessed.
There's a key concept behind this called

870af4ba-51bb-4229-8d02-12d91c82ddbc-2
00:10:37.204 --> 00:10:40.546
spaced repetition,
and spaced repetition is a study

870af4ba-51bb-4229-8d02-12d91c82ddbc-3
00:10:40.546 --> 00:10:45.687
technique where you revisit information
at set intervals in order to strengthen

870af4ba-51bb-4229-8d02-12d91c82ddbc-4
00:10:45.687 --> 00:10:49.350
the memory of it.
The idea is when you're revisiting the

870af4ba-51bb-4229-8d02-12d91c82ddbc-5
00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:52.563
piece of information,
you've almost forgotten it,

870af4ba-51bb-4229-8d02-12d91c82ddbc-6
00:10:52.563 --> 00:10:57.639
but you bring it back to the front of
your mind. So here you see the blue line

ed66d2c1-7df3-44ec-835b-377251d7421c-0
00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:02.193
is the forgetting curve.
If we don't revisit material over time,

ed66d2c1-7df3-44ec-835b-377251d7421c-1
00:11:02.193 --> 00:11:06.055
we tend to forget it.
But if we do revisit it one time,

ed66d2c1-7df3-44ec-835b-377251d7421c-2
00:11:06.055 --> 00:11:09.090
another time, 1/3 time, 1/4 time,
etcetera.

edce23a9-719f-4878-b2d0-d8ff045911f6-0
00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:15.681
And what we do is we increase our ability
to remember and recall and use that

edce23a9-719f-4878-b2d0-d8ff045911f6-1
00:11:15.681 --> 00:11:17.340
information over time.

ba3d4158-762c-443d-a117-a4d4c7ff6eb2-0
00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:26.157
Space repetition is a pretty old theory.
It has a ton of research over a very long

ba3d4158-762c-443d-a117-a4d4c7ff6eb2-1
